This work builds on, and does not modify, the following:

- Josh Alman and Virginia Vassilevska Williams, arXiv 2610.06783 (v1, October 2026): the thin-product theorem, the
  reductions to it, and every lemma used here (Theorem 17, Theorem thm:singletech, Corollary cor:tradeoff, Lemmas
  lem:realred and lem:cmp, the k-Clique folding, and the pruned recursion with its leaf count, lem:prune and
  lem:count, whose union of leaves Theorem 3 bounds), and the conclusion footnote that names the two routes made
  deterministic here. Their main theorems are Lean-formalised by Anthropic (anthropics/formal-math, 3sum-apsp).
- qawbecrdtey, github.com/qawbecrdtey/ImprovedExponents (published 2026-10-07, Lean): the exact leaf count and the
  pruned encodings (its Sections 3 and 4), used in every figure labelled "exact count + pruned encoding (after
  ImprovedExponents secs. 3-4)", and a Lean-checked (min,+) to all-edges Exact Triangle chain. We found the same re-tune
  improvements independently, but theirs came first. Its notes on limits also show that a random wanted set gives no
  saving over the worst-case leaf count and name structured (Hankel or anti-diagonal) wanted sets as an open lead;
  Theorem 3 pursues that lead, and the idea is theirs.
- Timothy M. Chan and Moshe Lewenstein, STOC 2015 (arXiv 1502.05204): the deterministic pseudo-perfect
  pseudo-additive family, built from small primes chosen one at a time by averaging over the survivors. Our chains
  follow this construction. Earlier: Jeanette P. Schmidt and Alan Siegel, SIAM J. Comput. 1990, and Noga Alon and
  Moni Naor, Algorithmica 1996, Sec. 4 (greedy covering by conditional expectations). Recent use: Nick Fischer,
  STOC 2026 (arXiv 2603.27736).
- Tsvi Kopelowitz, Seth Pettie and Ely Porat, SODA 2016: 3SUM to offline Set Disjointness with an AND over
  independent hashes, the reduction derandomised here.
- Timothy M. Chan and Yinzhan Xu, 2024 (arXiv 2310.11575): the deterministic hash-free bit halving and Fredman
  reduction for Exact Triangle (route L1), and the random-prime false-positive bound.
- Nick Fischer, Piotr Kaliciak and Adam Polak, ITCS 2024 (arXiv 2310.12913): interval groups (Lemma 3.3), the
  residue sets of their Set Disjointness reduction, and prime-at-a-time moduli (Lemma 3.2).
- Noga Alon and Moni Naor, Algorithmica 1996, Theorem 1: deterministic witnesses for Boolean products (route L1);
  Uri Zwick, JACM 2002, for their use beyond Boolean products.
- Virginia Vassilevska Williams and Ryan Williams (FOCS 2010, JACM 2018; and the 2013 negative-to-zero triangle
  reduction of SIAM J. Comput. 2013, Theorem 3.3): the (min,+) to Negative Triangle search and the dyadic reduction.
- Virginia Vassilevska Williams and Yinzhan Xu, 2020 (arXiv 2007.09318): the Monte Carlo Exact Triangle reduction
  (Theorem 3.4, Proposition 3.8) behind the Las Vegas remark.
- Timothy M. Chan, Virginia Vassilevska Williams and Yinzhan Xu, 2022 (arXiv 2203.08356): the comparison-count
  reductions used by route L1.
- Andreas Björklund, Thore Husfeldt, Petteri Kaski and Mikko Koivisto, 2010: trimmed Möbius inversion, the
  trimmed-transform idea behind pruned encodings.
- Michael L. Fredman, János Komlós and Endre Szemerédi, JACM 1984: choosing a hash prime deterministically by
  counting collisions (Theorem 3's residue buckets). The method of conditional expectations (Paul Erdős and John
  Selfridge 1973; Joel Spencer 1987; Prabhakar Raghavan 1988) for Theorem 3's shift, and Wassily Hoeffding 1963 for
  its typical signatures.
- Cited reductions for the corollaries: the Nešetřil-Poljak k-Clique folding (1985) as used in the paper, and
  Bremner et al. (BCD14) for (min,+)-convolution to (min,+) products.

What is new here:
- The survivor-restricted derandomisation of KPP16's AND (Theorem 1): the Chan-Lewenstein construction run with a
  per-prime criterion that counts the 3-term false positives of the surviving queries through the counting oracle,
  so that deterministic 3SUM keeps the whole thin-product saving.
- The all-edges weight H (Theorem 1, all-edges Exact Triangle with no scans); its small-prime zero test is folklore.
- The L1 composition (Theorem 2): Chan-Xu halving with the paper's counting oracle and Alon-Naor witnesses, with the
  3SUM window and the Alon-Naor bias repaired.
- The Las Vegas random-prime Exact Triangle variant (a remark; KPP16's amplification on the paper's instances).
- The folded residue instances (Theorem 1', proofs/stacked.md): the M residue instances of one Exact Triangle piece
  stacked into one square product, with the condition eps (E + gamma - 1/4) < 1; and its k-Clique values.
- The shared-leaf bound for the pruned recursion (Theorem 3, proofs/bands.md): the canonical-representative lemma and
  its generating function, the blocking laws of integer keys and the choice of the shift, the layout by segment
  signatures, the deterministic residue buckets with heavy-bucket recursion, the zone moves of Rule Z (Lemma Z), and
  the per-element window moves of Lemma Y with a periodic mix of segment lengths 8 and 9 (Theorem C'').
- The negative-to-zero triangle reduction written with the shift tau = 2h - w_ab, which fixes an off-by-one in our
  earlier version, and checked end to end.
- The arb certificates, the mechanical checks and the parameter points.

